**Answer: Yes**

**Proof.**

Recall that $H^{-1}(\Omega)$ is the dual space of $H^1_0(\Omega)$, i.e. the space of bounded linear functionals on $H^1_0(\Omega)$.

Let $u \in H^1(\Omega)$. We define $\Delta u$ in the distributional sense: for every test function $\varphi \in C_c^\infty(\Omega)$,

$$\langle \Delta u, \varphi \rangle := \int_\Omega u\, \Delta \varphi\, dx.$$

By integration by parts (Green's identity), and using that $\varphi$ is compactly supported in $\Omega$ (so all boundary terms vanish),

$$\int_\Omega u\, \Delta \varphi\, dx = -\int_\Omega \nabla u \cdot \nabla \varphi\, dx.$$

Since $u \in H^1(\Omega)$, we have $\nabla u \in L^2(\Omega)^n$. For any $\varphi \in H^1_0(\Omega)$, we have $\nabla \varphi \in L^2(\Omega)^n$. By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality,

$$\left| \int_\Omega \nabla u \cdot \nabla \varphi\, dx \right| \leq \|\nabla u\|_{L^2(\Omega)} \|\nabla \varphi\|_{L^2(\Omega)} \leq \|u\|_{H^1(\Omega)} \|\varphi\|_{H^1_0(\Omega)}.$$

Thus the linear functional

$$\varphi \mapsto -\int_\Omega \nabla u \cdot \nabla \varphi\, dx$$

is bounded on $H^1_0(\Omega)$, with operator norm at most $\|u\|_{H^1(\Omega)}$.

Since $C_c^\infty(\Omega)$ is dense in $H^1_0(\Omega)$, this bounded functional extends uniquely from $C_c^\infty(\Omega)$ to all of $H^1_0(\Omega)$. This extension is precisely the distributional Laplacian $\Delta u$, and it belongs to $H^{-1}(\Omega)$.

Therefore, for every $u \in H^1(\Omega)$, the distributional Laplacian $\Delta u \in H^{-1}(\Omega)$. $\blacksquare$

